   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>I was wondering whether it&#39;s possible to include latent traits in the lr.fixed formula with mixedmirt(). Specifically, I&#39;m interested in estimating the correlation between F1 and F2. For example:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;F1 = 1-23<br>                  F2 = 24-32&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>mirt1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mixedmirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> covdata </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> covdata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">random </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\">id</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mod</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> itemtype</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;graded&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Or would the COV option in the mirt.model() statement be what I need? Something like:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;F1 = 1-23<br>                  F2 = 24-32<br>                  COV = F1*F2)<br></span></div></code></div><br>Further, how could I see how the correlation between factors differs across groups? Thanks in advance!</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "skMNz-1oBQAJ",
